<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/earthquakes-and-seismology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Earthquakes are <strong>sudden releases of slowly stored plate strain</strong> — and the
    waves they generate are simultaneously the hazard and the instrument: the same signals that
    shake buildings let seismologists see inside the planet and, increasingly, warn before the
    shaking arrives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="rebound, waves, magnitude vs intensity, the destructive sequence" widget="equationStep" config={seisCfg} />

  <h2>The science that saves lives</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Earthquake early warning works:</strong> sensors detect P-waves and transmit alerts faster than damaging waves travel — Japan\'s system (Shinkansen brakes automatically) and Mexico City\'s buy 5–60 seconds; ShakeAlert now serves the US West Coast. Seconds stop trains and surgeries.</li>
    <li><strong>Prediction remains unsolved:</strong> nobody can say "quake on date X" — but forecasting (probabilities over decades: "30% chance of M≥6.7 by 2050") is mature and drives building codes, the real life-saver (Chile vs Haiti, 2010: similar magnitude, orders-of-magnitude different death tolls — codes and enforcement).</li>
    <li><strong>Paleoseismology extends the record:</strong> trenching faults to find prehistoric quakes — recurrence intervals for the Pacific Northwest\'s Cascadia megathrust (~500-year average, last 1700) come from drowned forests and Japanese tsunami records.</li>
    <li><strong>Induced seismicity is real:</strong> wastewater injection (not fracking itself, mostly) re-awakens faults — Oklahoma\'s quake rate rose ~300-fold then fell with regulation; human-caused, therefore human-manageable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Reid, elastic rebound (1910); USGS Earthquake Hazards Program; the 2004 and 2011 tsunami reports; ShakeAlert documentation.</p>
</TopicPage>
